WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.948
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.948 --> 00:00:02.709
Thursday the twenty-ninth.

00:00:02.709 --> 00:00:07.722
Calendar's simple today, a run and a workday, and the evening's wide open.

00:00:07.722 --> 00:00:10.228
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:10.228 --> 00:00:16.257
With an unguessable link, what's the real danger, someone guessing it, or something else?

00:00:16.257 --> 00:00:17.612
Answer's at the end.

00:00:17.612 --> 00:00:18.628
Weather's easy.

00:00:18.628 --> 00:00:24.385
Sunny and dry, high in the upper sixties, and tomorrow pushes into the low seventies.

00:00:24.385 --> 00:00:25.334
Okay, the day.

00:00:25.334 --> 00:00:29.330
Calendar has an easy zone two run at seven, thirty minutes.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.227
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:31.227 --> 00:00:33.327
After that, nothing's on there.

00:00:33.327 --> 00:00:36.917
So tonight's yours to do whatever, or nothing at all.

00:00:36.917 --> 00:00:37.865
Alright, news.

00:00:37.865 --> 00:00:40.101
Spain had a terrible train crash.

00:00:40.101 --> 00:00:48.432
A high-speed train ran into the back of a stopped train south of Madrid, near Toledo, and at least sixty-eight people died.

00:00:48.432 --> 00:00:56.764
The transport minister says a safety system failed, and that controllers then let a train move again without a speed limit.

00:00:56.764 --> 00:01:03.199
Several regions declared three days of mourning, and people are already demanding resignations.

00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:05.299
One from your corner of things.

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:06.925
Mistral, the French A.I.

00:01:06.925 --> 00:01:11.260
company, put out a new set of voice models called Voxtral three.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:21.149
There's transcription in over fifty languages, and a text to speech voice that covers about thirty-five languages and a pile of regional dialects.

00:01:21.149 --> 00:01:28.736
The part that stands out is that companies can run it on their own machines instead of renting it by the minute.

00:01:28.736 --> 00:01:38.219
Since this memo already runs on a voice model, it's one more option to keep in the back of your head if the current voice ever wears on you.

00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:39.303
And a happy one.

00:01:39.303 --> 00:01:45.941
The Milwaukee Brewers won the World Series last night, beating Tampa Bay four to one in game five.

00:01:45.941 --> 00:01:53.122
It's the first title in the team's history, after a regular season where they won more games than anybody.

00:01:53.122 --> 00:01:54.950
Here's one for the weekend.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:02:01.589
On Troutman Street, right by the Jefferson stop, there's a nonprofit thrift shop called Big Reuse.

00:02:01.589 --> 00:02:07.888
It opened earlier this year, and it's the small-stuff sibling of their big Gowanus warehouse.

00:02:07.888 --> 00:02:08.769
No furniture.

00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:14.662
Just clothes, books, records, toys, and a lot of home goods, like plates and glassware.

00:02:14.662 --> 00:02:17.033
That last part is the reason to go.

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:27.532
Mismatched plates, old glasses, and odd little dishes make cheap props and surfaces for product shots, and a few bucks there beats a prop store every time.

00:02:27.532 --> 00:02:30.309
It's open every day from eleven to seven.

00:02:30.309 --> 00:02:36.744
You can drop off donations while you're there, so it's a good excuse to clear out a shelf, too.

00:02:36.744 --> 00:02:39.047
Today's challenge is a social one.

00:02:39.047 --> 00:02:44.670
Send one message to someone you haven't talked to in a while, and make it specific.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:53.137
Not "hey, how's it going." Mention one real thing, a trip, a joke, a meal you had together, and say it popped into your head.

00:02:53.137 --> 00:02:54.763
It takes thirty seconds.

00:02:54.763 --> 00:02:58.759
People almost always like getting it more than you'd guess.

00:02:58.759 --> 00:03:01.265
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:01.265 --> 00:03:05.059
Remember picking this show's voice off that leaderboard?

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:11.832
You already know a hundred-point gap means the higher voice should win about two times out of three.

00:03:11.832 --> 00:03:14.271
Here's how the scores actually move.

00:03:14.271 --> 00:03:16.913
Every matchup starts with a prediction.

00:03:16.913 --> 00:03:24.228
Before a vote, the system works out how likely each voice is to win, based on the gap between their ratings.

00:03:24.228 --> 00:03:32.018
Then the vote comes in, and each voice's score moves by the difference between what happened and what was expected.

00:03:32.018 --> 00:03:34.524
So surprise is what moves the needle.

00:03:34.524 --> 00:03:43.398
If two voices are dead even and one wins, it was a coin flip, so the winner gains a fair chunk and the loser drops the same amount.

00:03:43.398 --> 00:03:48.614
If a heavy favorite wins, that's what everyone expected, and it barely moves.

00:03:48.614 --> 00:03:53.762
But if an underdog wins, it was a real surprise, and both scores swing hard.

00:03:53.762 --> 00:03:57.013
How hard is set by one number, usually called K.

00:03:57.013 --> 00:03:59.587
A big K means scores jump around fast.

00:03:59.587 --> 00:04:01.958
A small K means they settle slowly.

00:04:01.958 --> 00:04:04.803
Points don't appear out of nowhere either.

00:04:04.803 --> 00:04:07.648
Whatever one voice gains, the other loses.

00:04:07.648 --> 00:04:09.748
There's a catch with plain Elo.

00:04:09.748 --> 00:04:16.454
The order of the votes matters, so a new voice that gets lucky early can sit higher than it should.

00:04:16.454 --> 00:04:23.160
That's why a lot of arenas now look at all the votes together and fit one set of scores at the end.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.073
They also show a margin around each number.

00:04:26.073 --> 00:04:32.372
When two voices sit inside each other's margin, the leaderboard can't really tell them apart.

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:37.453
So when a new voice lands at the top by a few points, it's basically a tie.

00:04:37.453 --> 00:04:42.262
So: calendar says run at seven, Godinger till five, and a free evening.

00:04:42.262 --> 00:04:44.023
And send that one message.

00:04:44.023 --> 00:04:49.645
The answer: it's leaking, through chats, browser history, logs, or apps syncing it.

00:04:49.645 --> 00:04:51.000
Enjoy the sun, Ziad.
